window.PW={"version": "20261110a", "simple": {"john-3-16": {"title": "John 3:16: “God so loved the world”", "verse": {"tr": "web", "plain": {"t": "God loved the world, and he showed it by giving his one and only Son. Anyone who believes in him will not be lost. They will have life that never ends.", "refs": ["john-3-16.anglican.1", "john-3-16.orthodox.1"]}}, "big": {"t": "Did Jesus die for every person, or in a special way only for the people God [[chose|elect]]?", "refs": ["john-3-16.catholic.3", "john-3-16.reformed.3"]}, "agree": [{"point": "p1", "s": [{"t": "Orthodox, Catholic, Lutheran, Reformed, Anglican, and Baptist sources all use this verse to say God showed his love by giving his Son, and that people who believe receive eternal life.", "refs": ["john-3-16.orthodox.1", "john-3-16.catholic.1", "john-3-16.lutheran.1", "john-3-16.reformed.1", "john-3-16.anglican.1", "john-3-16.baptist.1"]}]}, {"point": "p3", "s": [{"t": "Orthodox, Catholic, Lutheran, and Reformed sources all call Jesus God’s “only-begotten” or “only” Son.", "refs": ["john-3-16.orthodox.3", "john-3-16.catholic.4", "john-3-16.lutheran.2", "john-3-16.reformed.1"]}, {"t": "The Orthodox Creed and the Catholic [[Catechism|catechism]] say this means he has always been God’s Son, from before time began.", "refs": ["john-3-16.orthodox.3", "john-3-16.catholic.4"]}]}], "differ_point": "p2", "differ_intro": null, "differ": [{"who": "Eastern Orthodox", "fam": ["orthodox"], "s": [{"t": "The Orthodox Communion prayer (the Divine Liturgy) says Christ gave himself “for the life of the world.” No official Orthodox ruling on this exact question was checked.", "refs": ["john-3-16.orthodox.2"]}]}, {"who": "Catholic", "fam": ["catholic"], "s": [{"t": "Christ died for every person, with no exceptions.", "refs": ["john-3-16.catholic.3"]}]}, {"who": "Lutheran", "fam": ["lutheran"], "group": "Protestant", "s": [{"t": "God’s promise in this verse is for all people.", "refs": ["john-3-16.lutheran.1"]}]}, {"who": "Anglican and Methodist (Wesleyan)", "fam": ["anglican", "wesleyan"], "group": "Protestant", "s": [{"t": "Both use almost the same words: Christ’s offering pays for “all the sins of the whole world.”", "refs": ["john-3-16.anglican.2", "john-3-16.wesleyan.1"]}]}, {"who": "Reformed", "fam": ["reformed"], "group": "Protestant", "s": [{"t": "Christ’s death is worth enough to cover the sins of the whole world, and the message should be told to everyone.", "refs": ["john-3-16.reformed.2", "john-3-16.reformed.3"]}, {"t": "But God meant it to truly save the people he [[chose|elect]], and only them.", "refs": ["john-3-16.reformed.3"]}]}, {"who": "Baptist", "fam": ["baptist"], "group": "Protestant", "s": [{"t": "Baptist statements don’t all agree. The Southern Baptist statement (2000) says salvation is offered freely to all.", "refs": ["john-3-16.baptist.1"]}, {"t": "The older 1689 Baptist Confession ties it to the people Christ won salvation for, and says it will surely reach every one of them.", "refs": ["john-3-16.baptist.2"]}]}], "scholars": [{"sq": 0, "s": [{"t": "[[Track A|track]] scholars checked here say the original word behind “only Son” means “only” or “unique,” not “only-begotten.”", "refs": ["john-3-16.s1A.1", "john-3-16.s1A.2"]}]}, {"sq": 2, "s": [{"t": "A [[Track B|track]] study found that what Jesus says in this chapter fits well with his teaching in the other Gospels.", "refs": ["john-3-16.s3B.1"]}]}]}, "matthew-16-18": {"title": "Matthew 16:18: Peter and “this rock”", "verse": {"tr": "web", "plain": {"t": "Jesus tells Simon that he is “Peter.” Jesus says he will build his church on “this rock,” and that “the gates of [[Hades|hades]]” will not win against it.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.orthodox.2", "matthew-16-18.catholic.3"]}}, "big": {"t": "Who or what is “the rock,” and does Peter’s special role pass down to the [[popes|pope]]?", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.catholic.1", "matthew-16-18.orthodox.1"]}, "agree": [{"point": "p1", "s": [{"t": "Orthodox, Catholic, and Lutheran sources all connect “the rock” with the faith Peter [[confessed|confess]] in the verses just before.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.orthodox.1", "matthew-16-18.catholic.5", "matthew-16-18.lutheran.1"]}]}, {"point": null, "s": [{"t": "A joint Catholic–Orthodox commission (2007) agrees that the church of Rome held first place among the early churches. They still disagree about what powers that gave its bishop. This statement is not official doctrine for either church.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.orthodox.3", "matthew-16-18.catholic.6"]}]}], "differ_point": "p2", "intro_where": "protestant", "differ_intro": {"t": "Protestant churches all reject the pope’s authority, but they explain “the rock” in different ways.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.lutheran.2", "matthew-16-18.reformed.2", "matthew-16-18.anglican.1", "matthew-16-18.baptist.1"]}, "differ": [{"who": "Eastern Orthodox", "fam": ["orthodox"], "s": [{"t": "The rock is Peter’s [[confession|confess]] of faith.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.orthodox.1", "matthew-16-18.orthodox.2"]}, {"t": "An 1848 letter from Orthodox leaders says Peter had no special power over the other apostles, “least of all in his successors.”", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.orthodox.1"]}]}, {"who": "Catholic", "fam": ["catholic"], "s": [{"t": "Peter is the rock. Jesus gave him authority over the whole church, and that role continues in every [[pope]].", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.catholic.1", "matthew-16-18.catholic.2", "matthew-16-18.catholic.3"]}, {"t": "This is [[dogma]], an official teaching Catholics are required to hold.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.catholic.1"]}, {"t": "The Catechism also says Peter is the rock because of the faith he confessed.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.catholic.4"]}]}, {"who": "Lutheran", "fam": ["lutheran"], "group": "Protestant", "s": [{"t": "The rock is the ministry of the faith Peter confessed. That ministry is not tied to one place or one person, so no single bishop inherits it.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.lutheran.1", "matthew-16-18.lutheran.2"]}]}, {"who": "Reformed", "fam": ["reformed"], "group": "Protestant", "s": [{"t": "Christ himself is the rock and the only head of the church. No apostle or successor is put in charge over the others.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.reformed.1", "matthew-16-18.reformed.2"]}]}, {"who": "Anglican", "fam": ["anglican"], "group": "Protestant", "s": [{"t": "Its official Articles say the pope has no authority over the Church of England. The texts checked don’t say who the rock is.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.anglican.1"]}]}, {"who": "Baptist", "fam": ["baptist"], "group": "Protestant", "s": [{"t": "The 1689 Baptist Confession says Christ is head of the church, and the pope is not, in any sense.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.baptist.1"]}, {"t": "The Southern Baptist statement uses this passage to talk about the local church, not about an office for Peter.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.baptist.2"]}]}], "scholars": [{"sq": 0, "s": [{"t": "[[Both tracks|track]] of scholars checked here agree: these verses give Peter himself a real role and authority in Matthew’s story.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.s1A.1", "matthew-16-18.s1B.1"]}]}, {"sq": 1, "s": [{"t": "[[Track A|track]]: “the gates of Hades” means evil spiritual powers, and Christ promises to protect the church from them.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.s2A.1"]}, {"t": "[[Track B|track]]: it is a figure of speech for death, which could not keep Christ locked up.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.s2B.1"]}]}, {"sq": 2, "s": []}]}, "belief-papal-primacy": {"title": "Does Peter’s role continue in the pope?", "big": {"t": "Did Jesus give Peter a leading role over the church that passes down to every [[pope]] (the bishop of Rome)?", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.catholic.1", "matthew-16-18.catholic.2", "matthew-16-18.orthodox.1"]}, "agree": [{"point": null, "s": [{"t": "A joint Catholic–Orthodox commission (2007) agrees that the church of Rome held first place among the early churches. They still disagree about what powers that gave its bishop.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.orthodox.3", "matthew-16-18.catholic.6"]}]}], "differ_point": "p2", "intro_where": "top", "differ_intro": {"t": "The Orthodox and Protestant sources checked all say no, each for its own reasons.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.orthodox.1", "matthew-16-18.lutheran.2", "matthew-16-18.reformed.2", "matthew-16-18.anglican.1", "matthew-16-18.baptist.1"]}, "differ": [{"who": "Eastern Orthodox", "fam": ["orthodox"], "s": [{"t": "No. An 1848 letter from Orthodox leaders says Peter had no special power over the other apostles, “least of all in his successors.”", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.orthodox.1"]}]}, {"who": "Catholic", "fam": ["catholic"], "s": [{"t": "Yes. Jesus gave Peter authority over the whole church, and that role continues in every pope.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.catholic.1", "matthew-16-18.catholic.2", "matthew-16-18.catholic.3"]}, {"t": "This is [[dogma]], an official teaching Catholics are required to hold.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.catholic.1"]}]}, {"who": "Lutheran", "fam": ["lutheran"], "group": "Protestant", "s": [{"t": "No. The church’s ministry is not tied to one place or one person, so no single bishop inherits Peter’s role.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.lutheran.2"]}]}, {"who": "Reformed", "fam": ["reformed"], "group": "Protestant", "s": [{"t": "No. Christ is the only head of the church, and no apostle or successor is put in charge over the others.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.reformed.2"]}]}, {"who": "Anglican", "fam": ["anglican"], "group": "Protestant", "s": [{"t": "No. Its official Articles say the pope has no authority over the Church of England.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.anglican.1"]}]}, {"who": "Baptist", "fam": ["baptist"], "group": "Protestant", "s": [{"t": "No. The 1689 Baptist Confession says the pope is not head of the church in any sense.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.baptist.1"]}]}], "scholars": [{"sq": null, "s": [{"t": "Neither track has a checked journal article on this question. One [[Track B|track]] article says plainly that it does not address the papacy.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.s1B.1"]}]}]}, "belief-extent-atonement": {"title": "Who did Jesus die for?", "big": {"t": "Did Jesus die for every single person, or in a special way only for the people God [[chose|elect]]? People call this the question of the [[atonement]].", "refs": ["john-3-16.catholic.3", "john-3-16.reformed.3"]}, "agree": [], "differ_point": "p2", "differ_intro": null, "differ": [{"who": "Eastern Orthodox", "fam": ["orthodox"], "s": [{"t": "The Orthodox Communion prayer (the Divine Liturgy) says Christ gave himself “for the life of the world.” No official Orthodox ruling on this exact question was checked.", "refs": ["john-3-16.orthodox.2"]}]}, {"who": "Catholic", "fam": ["catholic"], "s": [{"t": "Christ died for every person, with no exceptions.", "refs": ["john-3-16.catholic.3"]}]}, {"who": "Lutheran", "fam": ["lutheran"], "group": "Protestant", "s": [{"t": "God’s promise in this verse is for all people.", "refs": ["john-3-16.lutheran.1"]}]}, {"who": "Anglican and Methodist (Wesleyan)", "fam": ["anglican", "wesleyan"], "group": "Protestant", "s": [{"t": "Both use almost the same words: Christ’s offering pays for “all the sins of the whole world.”", "refs": ["john-3-16.anglican.2", "john-3-16.wesleyan.1"]}]}, {"who": "Reformed", "fam": ["reformed"], "group": "Protestant", "s": [{"t": "Christ’s death is worth enough to cover the sins of the whole world, and the message should be told to everyone.", "refs": ["john-3-16.reformed.2", "john-3-16.reformed.3"]}, {"t": "But God meant it to truly save the people he [[chose|elect]], and only them.", "refs": ["john-3-16.reformed.3"]}]}, {"who": "Baptist", "fam": ["baptist"], "group": "Protestant", "s": [{"t": "Baptist statements don’t all agree. The Southern Baptist statement (2000) says salvation is offered freely to all.", "refs": ["john-3-16.baptist.1"]}, {"t": "The older 1689 Baptist Confession ties it to the people Christ won salvation for, and says it will surely reach every one of them.", "refs": ["john-3-16.baptist.2"]}]}], "scholars": []}}, "glossary": {"elect": {"word": "Chosen (“the elect”)", "t": "In Reformed teaching, “the elect” are the people God chose to save before time began.", "refs": ["john-3-16.reformed.3"]}, "atonement": {"word": "Atonement", "t": "What Jesus’ death does about human sin. Churches describe it with words like redemption and paying for sins.", "refs": ["john-3-16.anglican.2", "john-3-16.catholic.3"]}, "catechism": {"word": "Catechism", "t": "The Catholic Church’s official book that explains its teaching.", "refs": ["john-3-16.catholic.1"]}, "dogma": {"word": "Dogma", "t": "A teaching the Catholic Church has officially defined, so Catholics must accept it. The First Vatican Council (1870) defined Peter’s role this way.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.catholic.1"]}, "pope": {"word": "Pope", "t": "The bishop of Rome, the leader of the Catholic Church. Catholic teaching says each pope succeeds Peter.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.catholic.2"]}, "confess": {"word": "Confess (a confession of faith)", "t": "Here it means saying out loud what you believe. Peter’s confession is when he tells Jesus who he is, just before this verse.", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.orthodox.1"]}, "hades": {"word": "Hades", "t": "A Greek name for the world of the dead. What “the gates of Hades” means here is debated by scholars (see What scholars say).", "refs": ["matthew-16-18.s2A.1", "matthew-16-18.s2B.1"]}, "track": {"word": "Track A and Track B", "t": "Two groups of scholar journals that this app keeps apart. Track A is historical-critical journals. Track B is evangelical academic journals. They are never blended, and neither is treated as the final word.", "refs": ["method:Track A: historical-critical journals", "method:Track B: evangelical academic journals", "method:Never average them"]}}, "reviewed": "9 November 2026", "sources": {"web": {"title": "World English Bible (WEB)", "url": "https://bible-api.com/john+3:16?translation=web", "license": "Public domain", "kind": "bible"}, "kjv": {"title": "King James Version (KJV, 1769 text)", "url": "https://bible-api.com/john+3:16?translation=kjv", "license": "Public domain (outside the UK Crown patent)", "kind": "bible"}, "asv": {"title": "American Standard Version (ASV, 1901)", "url": "https://bible-api.com/john+3:16?translation=asv", "license": "Public domain", "kind": "bible"}, "sblgnt": {"title": "SBL Greek New Testament (SBLGNT), via MorphGNT", "url": "https://github.com/morphgnt/sblgnt", "license": "CC BY 4.0 (https://sblgnt.com/license/); critical sigla removed", "kind": "bible"}, "ccc219": {"title": "Catechism of the Catholic Church §219", "url": "https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/__P16.HTM", "license": "© Libreria Editrice Vaticana; short excerpt"}, "ccc444": {"title": "Catechism of the Catholic Church §444", "url": "https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/__P1G.HTM", "license": "© LEV; short excerpt"}, "ccc458": {"title": "Catechism of the Catholic Church §458", "url": "https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/__P1J.HTM", "license": "© LEV; short excerpt"}, "ccc605": {"title": "Catechism of the Catholic Church §605", "url": "https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/__P1O.HTM", "license": "© LEV; short excerpt"}, "ccc424": {"title": "Catechism of the Catholic Church §424", "url": "https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/__P1D.HTM", "license": "© LEV; short excerpt"}, "ccc552": {"title": "Catechism of the Catholic Church §§552–553", "url": "https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/__P1L.HTM", "license": "© LEV; short excerpt"}, "ccc881": {"title": "Catechism of the Catholic Church §§881–882", "url": "https://www.vatican.va/archive/ENG0015/__P2A.HTM", "license": "© LEV; short excerpt"}, "vat1": {"title": "First Vatican Council (1870), Pastor aeternus, chs. 1–2 (Tanner translation)", "url": "https://www.papalencyclicals.net/councils/ecum20.htm", "license": "Translation in copyright; short excerpt"}, "goa_lit": {"title": "Divine Liturgy of St John Chrysostom (Greek Orthodox Archdiocese of America text)", "url": "https://www.goarch.org/-/the-divine-liturgy-of-saint-john-chrysostom", "license": "Translation © GOA; short excerpt"}, "enc1848": {"title": "Encyclical of the Eastern Patriarchs (1848), §11", "url": "https://www.orthodoxinfo.com/ecumenism/encyc_1848.aspx", "license": "19th-c. text; short excerpt"}, "chrys54": {"title": "John Chrysostom, Homily 54 on Matthew, §§2–3 (NPNF1 vol. 10)", "url": "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/200154.htm", "license": "Public domain translation (1888)"}, "ravenna": {"title": "Catholic–Orthodox Joint International Commission, Ravenna Document (2007), §41", "url": "https://www.christianunity.va/content/unitacristiani/en/dialoghi/sezione-orientale/chiese-ortodosse-di-tradizione-bizantina/commissione-mista-internazionale-per-il-dialogo-teologico-tra-la/documenti-di-dialogo/testo-in-inglese.html", "license": "Short excerpt"}, "fc_sd11": {"title": "Formula of Concord, Solid Declaration XI (Election), §§28, 67 (Triglot Concordia, 1921)", "url": "https://bookofconcord.org/solid-declaration/election/", "license": "Public domain translation"}, "treatise": {"title": "Treatise on the Power and Primacy of the Pope (Smalcald, 1537), §§22–29 (Triglot Concordia, 1921)", "url": "https://bookofconcord.org/power-and-primacy/treatise-compiled-at-smalcald/", "license": "Public domain translation"}, "dort": {"title": "Canons of Dort (1618–19), Heads I–II (Schaff, Creeds of Christendom vol. 3)", "url": "https://www.ccel.org/ccel/schaff/creeds3.iv.xvi.html", "license": "Public domain"}, "helv2": {"title": "Second Helvetic Confession, chs. XVII–XVIII (CCEL text)", "url": "https://www.ccel.org/creeds/helvetic.htm", "license": "Translation may be in copyright; short excerpt"}, "bcp1662": {"title": "Book of Common Prayer (1662), Holy Communion: the ‘Comfortable Words’", "url": "https://www.eskimo.com/~lhowell/bcp1662/communion/index.html", "license": "Public domain text (transcription)"}, "art39": {"title": "Thirty-nine Articles (BCP 1662), Articles XXXI and XXXVII", "url": "https://www.eskimo.com/~lhowell/bcp1662/articles/articles.html", "license": "Public domain text (transcription)"}, "bfm2000": {"title": "Baptist Faith and Message 2000 (Southern Baptist Convention), Arts. IV–VI", "url": "https://bfm.sbc.net/bfm2000/", "license": "Short excerpt"}, "lbcf8": {"title": "Second London Baptist Confession (1689), ch. 8 §8 (modern-spelling edition)", "url": "https://www.the1689confession.com/1689/chapter-8", "license": "Public domain confession; modernized text"}, "lbcf26": {"title": "Second London Baptist Confession (1689), ch. 26 §4 (modern-spelling edition)", "url": "https://www.the1689confession.com/1689/chapter-26", "license": "Public domain confession; modernized text"}, "umc_art": {"title": "Methodist Articles of Religion, Article XX (United Methodist Church text)", "url": "https://www.umc.org/en/content/articles-of-religion", "license": "Public domain articles (1784)"}, "aug124": {"title": "Augustine, Tractate 124 on John, §5 (NPNF1 vol. 7)", "url": "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1701124.htm", "license": "Public domain translation (1888)"}, "moody": {"title": "Dale Moody, “God’s Only Son: The Translation of John 3:16 in the RSV,” Journal of Biblical Literature 72.4 (1953) 213–19 — read via the abridgment printed with JBL’s permission in The Bible Translator 10.4 (1959)", "url": "https://translation.bible/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/moody-1959-god-s-only-son-the-translation-of-john-3-16-in-the-revised-standard-version.pdf", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.2307/3261699", "license": "Short excerpt", "track": "A"}, "pendrick": {"title": "Gerard Pendrick, “ΜΟΝΟΓΕΝΗΣ,” New Testament Studies 41 (1995) 587–600 — publisher extract only", "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/new-testament-studies/article/abs/monoenh/8C093FAB8D6B880BD116821B4EE1F112", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0028688500021718", "license": "Short excerpt", "track": "A"}, "jensen": {"title": "Aaron Michael Jensen, “The Syntax of the Correlative οὕτως … ὥστε in John 3.16…,” New Testament Studies (2019) — publisher abstract and notes only", "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/new-testament-studies/article/abs/syntax-of-the-correlative-in-john-316-in-the-light-of-parallel-constructions-in-the-ancient-greek-corpus/1A1C4264918AAF1EB10BAB8CA73A1BE8", "license": "Short excerpt", "track": "A"}, "kim": {"title": "Seon Yong Kim, “Ancient Binding Spells, Amulets and Matt 16.18–19,” New Testament Studies 62.3 (2016) 378–97 — publisher abstract only", "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/new-testament-studies/article/abs/ancient-binding-spells-amulets-and-matt-161819-revisiting-august-dells-proposal-a-century-later/C6BCE92DD0E52CFD5B8871458D67ED78", "license": "Short excerpt", "track": "A"}, "bartholoma": {"title": "Philipp Fabian Bartholomä, “The Johannine Discourses and the Teaching of Jesus in the Synoptics” (dissertation summary), Tyndale Bulletin 62.1 (2011)", "url": "https://www.tyndalebulletin.org/article/29312.pdf", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.53751/001c.29312", "license": "Open access; short excerpt", "track": "B"}, "ridlehoover": {"title": "Charles Nathan Ridlehoover, “The Matthean Peter,” Journal of the Evangelical Theological Society 64.4 (2021) 729–44", "url": "https://etsjets.org/wp-content/uploads/2010/02/files_JETS-PDFs_64_64-4_JETS_64.4_729-744_Ridlehoover.pdf", "license": "Free PDF; short excerpt", "track": "B"}, "lewis": {"title": "Jack P. Lewis, “‘The Gates of Hell Shall Not Prevail Against It’ (Matt 16:18): A Study of the History of Interpretation,” Journal of the Evangelical Theological Society 38.3 (1995) 349–67", "url": "https://etsjets.org/wp-content/uploads/2010/07/files_JETS-PDFs_38_38-3_38-3-pp349-367_JETS.pdf", "license": "Free PDF; short excerpt", "track": "B"}}, "queries": [{"id": "john-3-16", "type": "verse", "ref": "John 3:16", "title": "John 3:16 — “God so loved the world”", "aliases": ["john 3:16", "jn 3:16", "john 3 16", "jn3:16", "john3:16", "john 3", "god so loved the world"], "text": {"key": "john3_16", "focus": 16, "context": [14, 15, 16, 17], "translations": ["web", "kjv", "asv"], "original": "sblgnt", "note": "The English versions differ at one word: “one and only Son” (WEB) against “only begotten Son” (KJV, ASV). The Greek word is μονογενῆ (monogenē). See Scholar question 1 for what the opened journal sources say about it."}, "summary": "Traditions in this corpus agree on the core of the verse. They part ways on a follow-on question the verse is often used for: whether Christ’s death was for every person or effectually for the elect.", "points": [{"id": "p1", "q": "God’s love is shown in the gift of the Son, and those who believe receive eternal life", "cells": {"orthodox": "affirms", "catholic": "affirms", "lutheran": "affirms", "reformed": "affirms", "anglican": "affirms", "baptist": "affirms", "wesleyan": "none"}}, {"id": "p2", "q": "Christ’s death is for every person, not only for the elect", "cells": {"orthodox": "wording", "catholic": "affirms", "lutheran": "affirms", "reformed": "distinguishes", "anglican": "affirms", "baptist": "differ", "wesleyan": "affirms"}}, {"id": "p3", "q": "“Only (begotten) Son” names the Son’s eternal divine sonship", "cells": {"orthodox": "affirms", "catholic": "affirms", "lutheran": "affirms", "reformed": "affirms", "anglican": "none", "baptist": "none", "wesleyan": "none"}}], "traditions": {"orthodox": {"position": "Prays John 3:16 almost word for word at the heart of the Eucharistic prayer, and confesses the Son as “only-begotten” in the Creed.", "claims": [{"src": "goa_lit", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Liturgy in use (the Anaphora of St John Chrysostom)", "quote": "You so loved Your world that You gave Your only-begotten Son so that everyone who believes in Him should not perish, but have eternal life.", "points": ["p1"], "says": "The verse is addressed back to God in the Eucharistic prayer.", "verses": ["John 3:16"], "locator": "The Holy Anaphora", "id": "john-3-16.orthodox.1"}, {"src": "goa_lit", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Liturgy in use (same prayer, next sentence)", "quote": "when He delivered Himself up for the life of the world", "points": ["p2"], "says": "The liturgy speaks of Christ giving himself “for the life of the world.” No conciliar definition on the extent of the atonement was opened this run.", "verses": ["John 6:51 (echoed)"], "locator": "The Holy Anaphora", "id": "john-3-16.orthodox.2"}, {"src": "goa_lit", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Creed (Nicene–Constantinopolitan) as said in the liturgy", "quote": "the only-begotten Son of God, begotten of the Father before all ages", "points": ["p3"], "says": "The Creed links “only-begotten” to being begotten “of the Father before all ages”, that is, eternal sonship.", "locator": "The Symbol of Our Faith", "id": "john-3-16.orthodox.3"}], "oriental": "No Oriental Orthodox source was opened this run, so no difference is shown."}, "catholic": {"position": "Reads the verse as the revelation of God’s love in the Incarnation, and teaches that Christ died for all without exception.", "claims": [{"src": "ccc219", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Catechism (authoritative teaching text)", "quote": "his love will be victorious over even the worst infidelities and will extend to his most precious gift: \"God so loved the world that he gave his only Son.\"", "points": ["p1"], "says": "God’s covenant love culminates in the gift of the Son.", "verses": ["John 3:16", "Hos 11:1", "Is 49:14-15"], "locator": "§219, n. 40", "id": "john-3-16.catholic.1"}, {"src": "ccc458", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Catechism (authoritative teaching text)", "quote": "The Word became flesh so that thus we might know God's love", "points": ["p1"], "says": "Quotes 1 John 4:9 and John 3:16 as the reason for the Incarnation.", "verses": ["1 John 4:9", "John 3:16"], "locator": "§458, nn. 72–73", "id": "john-3-16.catholic.2"}, {"src": "ccc605", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Catechism, quoting the Council of Quiercy (853)", "quote": "The Church, following the apostles, teaches that Christ died for all men without exception", "points": ["p2"], "says": "Christ died for every human being; it also reads “ransom for many” as non-restrictive.", "verses": ["Mt 18:14", "Mt 20:28"], "argument": "On “a ransom for many”: the last term “is not restrictive, but contrasts the whole of humanity with the unique person of the redeemer”.", "locator": "§605", "id": "john-3-16.catholic.3"}, {"src": "ccc444", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Catechism (authoritative teaching text)", "quote": "Jesus calls himself the \"only Son of God\", and by this title affirms his eternal pre-existence.", "points": ["p3"], "says": "The title in John 3:16 is read as a claim to eternal pre-existence.", "verses": ["John 3:16", "John 10:36"], "locator": "§444, n. 54", "id": "john-3-16.catholic.4"}], "opinion_note": "Everything shown here is from the Catechism. No theological-opinion source on this verse was opened this run."}, "protestant": {"split": true, "split_reason": "Protestant confessions agree on the core of the verse, but they divide on whether Christ’s death was for every person or effectually for the elect only. So each family gets its own row.", "rows": [{"family": "Lutheran", "position": "The gospel promise in John 3:16 is universal: it is for all people.", "claims": [{"src": "fc_sd11", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Confession: Formula of Concord, Solid Declaration XI", "quote": "the promise of the Gospel is universalis (universal), that is, it pertains to all men", "points": ["p1", "p2"], "says": "Cites John 3:16 first in a chain of texts showing that the promise is for all.", "verses": ["John 3:16", "John 1:29", "John 6:51", "1 John 2:2", "Matt 11:28", "2 Pet 3:9"], "argument": "“For God loved the world and gave His Son, John 3:16. Christ bore the sins of the world, John 1:29.”", "locator": "§28", "id": "john-3-16.lutheran.1"}, {"src": "fc_sd11", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Confession: Formula of Concord, Solid Declaration XI", "quote": "Christ, as the only-begotten Son of God, who is in the bosom of the Father, has announced to us the will of the Father", "points": ["p3"], "says": "The “only-begotten Son” reveals the Father’s will, quoting John 3:16.", "verses": ["John 3:16", "Mark 1:15", "John 6:40"], "locator": "§67", "id": "john-3-16.lutheran.2"}]}, {"family": "Reformed", "position": "Christ’s death is enough for the whole world and the promise is preached to all, but it was meant to redeem the elect effectually.", "claims": [{"src": "dort", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Confession: Canons of Dort, Head I art. 2", "quote": "But 'in this the love of God was manifested, that he sent his only-begotten Son into the world,' 'that whosoever believeth on him should not perish, but have everlasting life'", "points": ["p1", "p3"], "says": "Opens the doctrine of predestination with John 3:16.", "verses": ["1 John 4:9", "John 3:16"], "locator": "Head I, art. 2", "id": "john-3-16.reformed.1"}, {"src": "dort", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Confession: Canons of Dort, Head II art. 3", "quote": "is of infinite worth and value, abundantly sufficient to expiate the sins of the whole world", "points": ["p2"], "says": "The death of Christ is sufficient for the whole world.", "locator": "Head II, art. 3", "id": "john-3-16.reformed.2"}, {"src": "dort", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Confession: Canons of Dort, Head II art. 8", "quote": "all those, and those only, who were from eternity chosen to salvation", "points": ["p2"], "says": "God’s purpose was that Christ should effectually redeem the elect.", "argument": "Art. 5 says the promise “ought to be declared and published to all nations, and to all persons promiscuously and without distinction.”", "locator": "Head II, art. 8", "id": "john-3-16.reformed.3"}]}, {"family": "Anglican", "position": "Uses John 3:16 in the Communion service, and its Articles call Christ’s offering satisfaction “for all the sins of the whole world.”", "claims": [{"src": "bcp1662", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Liturgy: Book of Common Prayer 1662, the “Comfortable Words”", "quote": "So God loved the world, that he gave his only-begotten Son, to the end that all that believe in him should not perish, but have everlasting life.", "points": ["p1"], "says": "John 3:16 is read aloud to communicants after the absolution.", "verses": ["John 3:16"], "locator": "Holy Communion", "id": "john-3-16.anglican.1"}, {"src": "art39", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Confession: Thirty-nine Articles, Art. XXXI", "quote": "offering of Christ once made is the perfect redemption, propitiation, and satisfaction for all the sins of the whole world", "points": ["p2"], "says": "Christ’s one offering is for the sins of the whole world.", "locator": "Art. XXXI", "id": "john-3-16.anglican.2"}]}, {"family": "Baptist", "position": "Baptist confessions differ: the BF&M 2000 stresses salvation offered freely to all; the 1689 Confession ties redemption to those for whom Christ obtained it.", "claims": [{"src": "bfm2000", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Confession: Baptist Faith and Message 2000, Art. IV (Southern Baptist)", "quote": "is offered freely to all who accept Jesus Christ as Lord and Saviour", "points": ["p1", "p2"], "says": "Lists John 3:3-21 among the texts for this article (Art. V also lists John 3:16).", "verses": ["John 3:3-21", "John 3:16"], "locator": "Arts. IV–V", "id": "john-3-16.baptist.1"}, {"src": "lbcf8", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Confession: Second London Baptist Confession (1689), 8.8 (Particular Baptist)", "quote": "To all those for whom Christ has obtained eternal redemption, He does certainly and effectually apply and communicate the same", "points": ["p2"], "says": "Redemption is applied to all those for whom it was obtained.", "verses": ["John 6:37", "John 10:15-16", "John 17:9"], "locator": "ch. 8 §8", "id": "john-3-16.baptist.2"}]}, {"family": "Pentecostal / Wesleyan", "position": "Methodist Articles call Christ’s offering satisfaction for “all the sins of the whole world.” No Pentecostal statement was opened (the Assemblies of God page blocked automated access).", "claims": [{"src": "umc_art", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Confession: Methodist Articles of Religion, Art. XX", "quote": "The offering of Christ, once made, is that perfect redemption, propitiation, and satisfaction for all the sins of the whole world", "points": ["p2"], "says": "Same wording family as Anglican Art. XXXI.", "locator": "Art. XX", "id": "john-3-16.wesleyan.1"}]}]}}, "scholar": {"coverage": "Track A: three opened items (one through an authorized abridgment, two as publisher extracts or abstracts only). Track B: one opened item, and it answers a different question. None of the journal full texts was paywall-free except the abridgment, so this section shows only what could be opened.", "questions": [{"q": "What does μονογενής (monogenēs) mean: “only/unique” or “only-begotten”?", "status": "one-track", "statement": "Only Track A was opened on this question. The Track A items opened read the word as “only” or “unique.”", "A": [{"src": "moody", "label": "Interpretation", "authority": "JBL 72 (1953), via the authorized abridgment", "quote": "the RSV has simply corrected an error repeated for fifteen centuries", "says": "“Only” is the linguistically correct sense. Moody also says Jerome changed the Old Latin unicus (“only”) to unigenitus (“only begotten”) and that this passed into the KJV.", "argument": "“In John 1:14, 18, 3:16, 18, Heb. 11:17, and 1 John 4:9 Jerome changed the Old Latin unicus to unigenitus.”", "id": "john-3-16.s1A.1"}, {"src": "pendrick", "label": "Interpretation", "authority": "NTS 41 (1995), publisher extract", "quote": "the adjective's usual meaning is in fact ‘unique’ or ‘only’, ‘single’", "says": "Argues from Greek usage that the sense is unique or only. The extract also says some authors had argued for “only-begotten” again, and its reference list names J. V. Dahms, NTS 29 (1983), as one of them. Dahms’s article itself was not opened.", "id": "john-3-16.s1A.2"}], "B": [], "B_gap": "No Track B article on μονογενής was opened. Search did not turn up a free article in Tyndale Bulletin, JETS, BBR, WTJ or Bibliotheca Sacra; anything in BBR or BibSac would sit behind a subscription."}, {"q": "Does οὕτως (“so”) in John 3:16 mean “so much” (how much) or “in this way” (how)?", "status": "one-track", "statement": "Only Track A was opened. Even within Track A the opened sources disagree, so both sides are shown and not averaged.", "A": [{"src": "jensen", "label": "Interpretation", "authority": "NTS (2019), publisher abstract", "quote": "functions as a correlative intensifier–result pair, exactly as it is presented in the traditional understanding", "says": "Defends the traditional reading (God loved the world so much that he gave…) from parallel Greek constructions.", "id": "john-3-16.s2A.1"}, {"src": "jensen", "label": "Interpretation", "authority": "Same item, note 2 (reporting the other view)", "quote": "Gundry, R. and Howell, R., ‘The Sense and Syntax of John 3:14–17 with Special Reference to the Use of οὕτως… ὥστε in John 3:16’, NovT 41 (1999): 24–39", "says": "Jensen names Gundry and Howell (Novum Testamentum, Track A) among those who read it otherwise. Their article itself was not opened (Brill paywall), so their argument is not quoted here.", "id": "john-3-16.s2A.2"}], "B": [], "B_gap": "No Track B article on this question was opened."}, {"q": "Does the Nicodemus discourse (John 3:1–21) reliably represent Jesus’ own teaching?", "status": "one-track", "statement": "Only Track B was opened on this question.", "A": [], "A_gap": "No Track A article on this question was opened.", "B": [{"src": "bartholoma", "label": "History", "authority": "Tyndale Bulletin 62.1 (2011), dissertation summary", "quote": "a significant coherence in meaning and conceptual substance between what Jesus said to Nicodemus and his teaching in the Synoptics", "says": "Finds little verbal overlap but strong conceptual overlap, including on John 3:16–18 (“Jesus as the Saviour who gives eternal life to those who believe”).", "verses": ["John 3:16-18", "Matt 19:28", "Luke 19:10"], "id": "john-3-16.s3B.1"}]}]}, "history": null, "canon": null}, {"id": "matthew-16-18", "type": "verse", "ref": "Matthew 16:18", "title": "Matthew 16:18 — Peter and the rock", "aliases": ["matthew 16:18", "matt 16:18", "mt 16:18", "matthew 16", "mt16:18", "peter and the rock", "upon this rock", "on this rock", "peter rock", "you are peter", "thou art peter"], "text": {"key": "matt16_18", "focus": 18, "context": [15, 16, 17, 18, 19], "translations": ["web", "kjv", "asv"], "original": "sblgnt", "note": "In the Greek, “Peter” is Πέτρος (Petros) and “rock” is πέτρᾳ (petra). The two words differ in form but share a root. Traditions read that wordplay differently, as shown below."}, "summary": "This is a real split. Catholic dogma identifies Peter as the rock and holds that his primacy continues in the bishop of Rome. Orthodox and Protestant sources in this corpus reject that jurisdiction, and they give different readings of the rock: Peter’s confession, its ministry, or Christ.", "points": [{"id": "p1", "q": "The “rock” is (or includes) the faith Peter confessed", "cells": {"orthodox": "affirms", "catholic": "both", "lutheran": "affirms", "reformed": "christ", "anglican": "none", "baptist": "none", "wesleyan": "none"}}, {"id": "p2", "q": "Peter’s primacy of jurisdiction continues in the bishop of Rome", "cells": {"orthodox": "denies", "catholic": "dogma", "lutheran": "denies", "reformed": "denies", "anglican": "denies", "baptist": "denies", "wesleyan": "none"}}], "traditions": {"orthodox": {"position": "The rock is Peter’s confession of faith. Rome held first place in the ancient order of churches, but papal jurisdiction is rejected.", "claims": [{"src": "enc1848", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Synodal encyclical of the Eastern patriarchs (official; not an ecumenical council)", "quote": "the Church of Christ was founded upon the immovable rock of St. Peter’s Confession", "points": ["p1", "p2"], "says": "Otherwise, it says, “the Church was built upon a slippery foundation, even on Cephas himself, not to say on the Pope”.", "verses": ["Matt 16:15-16", "John 21:16"], "argument": "It says the confession was “the answer of the Apostles in common,” and that “Feed my sheep” “implied no prerogative in St. Peter over the other Apostles, least of all in his successors.”", "locator": "§11", "id": "matthew-16-18.orthodox.1"}, {"src": "chrys54", "label": "Interpretation", "authority": "Church Father: John Chrysostom (a theologoumenon, not a conciliar dogma)", "quote": "upon this rock will I build my Church; Matthew 16:18 that is, on the faith of his confession", "points": ["p1"], "says": "Reads the rock as the faith Peter confessed. In the same homily Christ also makes Peter “a shepherd” and gives him the keys.", "verses": ["Matt 16:16-19"], "locator": "Hom. 54 §3", "id": "matthew-16-18.orthodox.2"}, {"src": "ravenna", "label": "History", "authority": "Joint Catholic–Orthodox dialogue statement (not dogma for either church)", "quote": "Rome, as the Church that “presides in love” according to the phrase of St Ignatius of Antioch (To the Romans, Prologue), occupied the first place in the taxis", "points": ["p2"], "says": "Both sides agree Rome held first place in the ancient order. They “disagree, however, on the interpretation of the historical evidence” about the bishop of Rome’s prerogatives.", "locator": "§41", "id": "matthew-16-18.orthodox.3"}], "dogma_note": "No ecumenical-council definition on how to read this verse was found in the Orthodox sources opened. The 1848 encyclical is an official synodal letter. Chrysostom’s reading is patristic opinion (theologoumenon).", "oriental": "No Oriental Orthodox source was opened this run, so no difference is shown."}, "catholic": {"position": "Defined dogma: Christ promised and gave Peter a primacy of jurisdiction, and it continues in the bishop of Rome. The Catechism also speaks of the faith Peter confessed as the rock.", "claims": [{"src": "vat1", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Defined dogma: First Vatican Council, Pastor aeternus ch. 1 (with canon)", "quote": "a primacy of jurisdiction over the whole church of God was immediately and directly promised to the blessed apostle Peter and conferred on him by Christ the lord", "points": ["p2"], "says": "Quotes Matt 16:17–19 as the promise and John 21 as the conferral, and anathematizes denial.", "verses": ["Matt 16:17-19", "John 1:42", "John 21:15-17"], "locator": "ch. 1", "id": "matthew-16-18.catholic.1"}, {"src": "vat1", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Defined dogma: First Vatican Council, Pastor aeternus ch. 2", "quote": "whoever succeeds to the chair of Peter obtains by the institution of Christ himself, the primacy of Peter over the whole church", "points": ["p2"], "says": "Peter’s primacy has perpetual successors in the Roman pontiffs.", "locator": "ch. 2", "id": "matthew-16-18.catholic.2"}, {"src": "ccc881", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Catechism (authoritative teaching text)", "quote": "The Lord made Simon alone, whom he named Peter, the \"rock\" of his Church.", "points": ["p2"], "says": "Peter is the rock. The Catechism cites Matt 16:18-19 and John 21:15-17, and says this office continues “under the primacy of the Pope.”", "verses": ["Matt 16:18-19", "John 21:15-17"], "locator": "§881, n. 400", "id": "matthew-16-18.catholic.3"}, {"src": "ccc552", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Catechism (authoritative teaching text)", "quote": "Because of the faith he confessed Peter will remain the unshakeable rock of the Church.", "points": ["p1"], "says": "Peter is the rock because of the faith he confessed.", "verses": ["Matt 16:18", "1 Pet 2:4", "Luke 22:32"], "locator": "§552", "id": "matthew-16-18.catholic.4"}, {"src": "ccc424", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Catechism (authoritative teaching text, citing Leo the Great)", "quote": "On the rock of this faith confessed by St. Peter, Christ built his Church.", "points": ["p1"], "says": "Catholic teaching also uses the faith-confession reading alongside Peter-as-rock.", "verses": ["Matt 16:16", "Matt 16:18"], "locator": "§424, n. 9", "id": "matthew-16-18.catholic.5"}, {"src": "ravenna", "label": "History", "authority": "Joint Catholic–Orthodox dialogue statement (not dogma for either church)", "quote": "They disagree, however, on the interpretation of the historical evidence from this era regarding the prerogatives of the bishop of Rome as protos", "points": ["p2"], "says": "The same dialogue text shown in the Orthodox column, from the other side of the table.", "locator": "§41", "id": "matthew-16-18.catholic.6"}], "opinion_note": "Magisterial: Vatican I (defined, with anathema) and the Catechism. As theological opinion, see the history row for Augustine, who left the reader to choose between “Peter” and “Christ” as the rock."}, "protestant": {"split": true, "split_reason": "Protestant confessions agree in rejecting papal jurisdiction, but they read the rock differently and give different reasons. So each family gets its own row.", "rows": [{"family": "Lutheran", "position": "The rock is the ministry of the confession Peter made. That ministry is not tied to one place or person.", "claims": [{"src": "treatise", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Confession: Treatise on the Power and Primacy of the Pope (Book of Concord)", "quote": "the Church has not been built upon the authority of man, but upon the ministry of the confession which Peter made", "points": ["p1"], "says": "“Upon this rock, i.e., upon this ministry.”", "verses": ["Matt 16:18-19", "John 21:15"], "locator": "§25", "id": "matthew-16-18.lutheran.1"}, {"src": "treatise", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Confession: Treatise on the Power and Primacy of the Pope", "quote": "the ministry of the New Testament is not bound to places and persons as the Levitical ministry", "points": ["p2"], "says": "So no single see inherits it.", "argument": "Appeals to the Fathers: “not as referring to the person of Peter, most of the holy Fathers, as Origen, Cyprian, Augustine” (and Hilary and Bede) read the passage; and quotes Chrysostom: “He built His Church not upon man, but upon the faith of Peter.”", "locator": "§26", "id": "matthew-16-18.lutheran.2"}]}, {"family": "Reformed", "position": "Christ alone is the Church’s rock and head. No apostle or successor holds primacy.", "claims": [{"src": "helv2", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Confession: Second Helvetic Confession, ch. XVII", "quote": "It does not err as long as it rests upon the rock Christ", "points": ["p1"], "says": "Names Christ as the rock the Church rests on. (This sentence does not cite Matt 16:18 directly.)", "verses": ["1 Cor 10:4 (cited elsewhere in the confession)"], "locator": "ch. XVII", "id": "matthew-16-18.reformed.1"}, {"src": "helv2", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Confession: Second Helvetic Confession, ch. XVII", "quote": "we do not approve of the doctrine of the Roman clergy, who make their Pope at Rome the universal shepherd and supreme head of the Church Militant here on earth", "points": ["p2"], "says": "Christ is the “sole head”; “He has strictly forbidden his apostles and their successors to have any primacy and dominion in the Church.”", "verses": ["Col 1:18", "Eph 5:23", "Eph 1:22", "Eph 4:15"], "argument": "Ch. XVIII says Peter presided at Jerusalem but “was not on that account preferred to the others, nor endowed with greater authority than the rest.”", "locator": "ch. XVII", "id": "matthew-16-18.reformed.2"}]}, {"family": "Anglican", "position": "The Articles reject the bishop of Rome’s jurisdiction in England. No reading of the rock was found in the texts opened.", "claims": [{"src": "art39", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Confession: Thirty-nine Articles, Art. XXXVII", "quote": "The Bishop of Rome hath no jurisdiction in this realm of England", "points": ["p2"], "says": "A jurisdictional denial. The article does not interpret Matt 16:18.", "locator": "Art. XXXVII", "id": "matthew-16-18.anglican.1"}]}, {"family": "Baptist", "position": "Christ is the head of the church, not the Pope. The BF&M cites Matt 16:15–19 for the local congregation.", "claims": [{"src": "lbcf26", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Confession: Second London Baptist Confession (1689), 26.4", "quote": "neither can the Pope of Rome in any sense be head thereof", "points": ["p2"], "says": "Christ is head of the church. (The paragraph goes on in stronger polemical language, not quoted here.)", "locator": "ch. 26 §4", "id": "matthew-16-18.baptist.1"}, {"src": "bfm2000", "label": "Official church teaching", "authority": "Confession: Baptist Faith and Message 2000, Art. VI", "quote": "A New Testament church of the Lord Jesus Christ is an autonomous local congregation of baptized believers", "points": [], "says": "Matt 16:15-19 heads the list of texts cited for this article. It is used for the church as a congregation, not for an office of Peter.", "verses": ["Matt 16:15-19", "Matt 18:15-20"], "locator": "Art. VI", "id": "matthew-16-18.baptist.2"}]}, {"family": "Pentecostal / Wesleyan", "position": "No confessional text addressing this verse or papal primacy was opened for these families this run.", "claims": []}]}}, "scholar": {"coverage": "One opened article per track: Track A a publisher abstract (NTS), Track B two free JETS PDFs. This is not a survey of either field. Other Track A articles on this passage were found but could not be opened: Wilcox, NTS 22 (1975), preview only with no abstract; Robinson, JSNT 6 (1984), page blocked; Seitz, JBL 69 (1950), paywall; Marcus, CBQ 50 (1988), paywall.", "questions": [{"q": "Does the passage give Peter himself a personal role and authority?", "status": "agree", "statement": "Both academic tracks teach that Matthew 16:18–19 gives a personal role and authority to Peter himself within Matthew’s text. Neither opened item goes on to argue about successors.", "A": [{"src": "kim", "label": "Interpretation", "authority": "NTS 62.3 (2016), publisher abstract", "quote": "crafted to highlight Peter's authority to ‘bind’ and ‘break’ whatever entities he should choose", "says": "Reads v. 19 against ancient binding-spell conventions: Peter as “key-bearer.”", "id": "matthew-16-18.s1A.1"}], "B": [{"src": "ridlehoover", "label": "Interpretation", "authority": "JETS 64.4 (2021)", "quote": "Peter’s role is as gatekeeper and he is the rock on which the church is built", "says": "Also reads Peter’s later rebuke (16:23) as foreshadowing a fall. It sets the papacy aside explicitly: “The following proposal will not address Peter and the papacy.”", "id": "matthew-16-18.s1B.1"}]}, {"q": "What are the “gates of Hades” that will not prevail?", "status": "disagree", "statement": "The tracks disagree here. Both are shown, labeled; they are not averaged.", "A": [{"src": "kim", "label": "Interpretation", "authority": "NTS 62.3 (2016), publisher abstract", "quote": "The security of the church against malevolent and harmful spiritual entities is further guaranteed by Christ's promise", "says": "Reads the promise as protecting the church against hostile spiritual powers.", "id": "matthew-16-18.s2A.1"}], "B": [{"src": "lewis", "label": "Interpretation", "authority": "JETS 38.3 (1995)", "quote": "be considered a figure of speech for death, which cannot keep the Christ imprisoned", "says": "After surveying the history of interpretation, Lewis rejects the “attack” reading. He reports Joel Marcus (CBQ 50, 1988, Track A) for that reading but calls the case “more astute than convincing.” Marcus’s article itself was not opened.", "verses": ["Acts 2:24", "Rom 6:9"], "id": "matthew-16-18.s2B.1"}]}, {"q": "Does Peter’s role pass to successors (the bishop of Rome)?", "status": "unsourced", "statement": "Neither track has an opened journal source on this question, so nothing is shown. Ridlehoover (Track B) sets it aside explicitly.", "A": [], "B": [], "A_gap": "Not opened.", "B_gap": "Not opened."}]}, "history": [{"era": "Early", "claims": [{"src": "lewis", "label": "History", "authority": "As reported in JETS 38.3 (1995) p. 359, quoting Hilary of Poitiers", "quote": "Upon this rock, then, of the confession, is the building of the Church", "says": "Lewis: Hilary “seems first to have introduced” the confession reading among Latin writers.", "id": "matthew-16-18.hist-early.1"}, {"src": "chrys54", "label": "History", "authority": "John Chrysostom, Hom. 54 on Matthew", "quote": "that is, on the faith of his confession", "says": "The rock is the faith of Peter’s confession.", "id": "matthew-16-18.hist-early.2"}, {"src": "aug124", "label": "History", "authority": "Augustine, Tractate 124 on John", "quote": "For petra (rock) is not derived from Peter, but Peter from petra", "says": "The rock is Christ, whom Peter confessed; Peter stands for the Church: “in this representation Christ is to be understood as the Rock, Peter as the Church.”", "id": "matthew-16-18.hist-early.3"}, {"src": "lewis", "label": "History", "authority": "As reported in JETS 38.3 (1995) p. 360, quoting Augustine’s Retractations", "quote": "Let the reader choose whatever of these two senses may be the more probable", "says": "Augustine leaves the choice open between “Peter” and “Christ whom Peter confessed.” Lewis also notes Augustine elsewhere used the verse for the see of Peter.", "id": "matthew-16-18.hist-early.4"}]}, {"era": "Medieval", "claims": [], "gap": "No medieval source was opened this run."}, {"era": "Reformation", "claims": [{"src": "treatise", "label": "History", "authority": "Lutheran, 1537", "quote": "Therefore the building of the Church is upon this rock of confession; this faith is the foundation of the Church.", "says": "See the Lutheran row.", "id": "matthew-16-18.hist-reformation.1"}]}, {"era": "Modern", "claims": [{"src": "enc1848", "label": "History", "authority": "Eastern patriarchs, 1848", "quote": "the immovable rock of St. Peter’s", "says": "See the Orthodox column.", "id": "matthew-16-18.hist-modern.1"}, {"src": "vat1", "label": "History", "authority": "First Vatican Council, 1870", "quote": "the primacy of Peter over the whole church", "says": "See the Catholic column.", "id": "matthew-16-18.hist-modern.2"}, {"src": "ravenna", "label": "History", "authority": "Catholic–Orthodox dialogue, 2007", "quote": "occupied the first place in the taxis", "says": "Agreement on Rome’s first place, disagreement on its prerogatives.", "id": "matthew-16-18.hist-modern.3"}]}], "canon": null}, {"id": "belief-papal-primacy", "type": "belief", "title": "Does Peter’s office of primacy continue in the bishop of Rome?", "aliases": ["papacy", "pope", "papal primacy", "primacy", "bishop of rome", "peter's successor", "successor of peter", "is the pope", "pope head of the church", "petrine", "vicar of christ"], "from_query": "matthew-16-18", "point": "p2", "summary": "This uses the same sourced texts as the Matthew 16:18 page, filtered to the question of primacy."}, {"id": "belief-extent-atonement", "type": "belief", "title": "For whom did Christ die: for every person, or effectually for the elect?", "aliases": ["atonement", "limited atonement", "definite atonement", "unlimited atonement", "died for all", "for whom did christ die", "christ died for", "christ die for", "die for everyone", "died for everyone", "die for all", "died for the elect", "particular redemption", "sins of the whole world"], "from_query": "john-3-16", "point": "p2", "summary": "This uses the same sourced texts as the John 3:16 page, filtered to the extent of the atonement."}], "bible": {"john3_16": {"web": {"name": "World English Bible", "note": "Public Domain", "verses": {"14": "As Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, even so must the Son of Man be lifted up,", "15": "that whoever believes in him should not perish, but have eternal life.", "16": "For God so loved the world, that he gave his one and only Son, that whoever believes in him should not perish, but have eternal life.", "17": "For God didn’t send his Son into the world to judge the world, but that the world should be saved through him."}, "source_url": "https://bible-api.com/john+3:14-17?translation=web"}, "kjv": {"name": "King James Version", "note": "Public Domain", "verses": {"14": "And as Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, even so must the Son of man be lifted up:", "15": "That whosoever believeth in him should not perish, but have eternal life.", "16": "For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son, that whosoever believeth in him should not perish, but have everlasting life.", "17": "For God sent not his Son into the world to condemn the world; but that the world through him might be saved."}, "source_url": "https://bible-api.com/john+3:14-17?translation=kjv"}, "asv": {"name": "American Standard Version (1901)", "note": "Public Domain", "verses": {"14": "And as Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, even so must the Son of man be lifted up;", "15": "that whosoever believeth may in him have eternal life.", "16": "For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son, that whosoever believeth on him should not perish, but have eternal life.", "17": "For God sent not the Son into the world to judge the world; but that the world should be saved through him."}, "source_url": "https://bible-api.com/john+3:14-17?translation=asv"}, "sblgnt": {"name": "SBL Greek New Testament (SBLGNT)", "note": "CC BY 4.0, Society of Biblical Literature and Logos Bible Software; text via MorphGNT (Tauber ed.), critical sigla removed", "verses": {"14": "καὶ καθὼς Μωϋσῆς ὕψωσεν τὸν ὄφιν ἐν τῇ ἐρήμῳ, οὕτως ὑψωθῆναι δεῖ τὸν υἱὸν τοῦ ἀνθρώπου,", "15": "ἵνα πᾶς ὁ πιστεύων ἐν αὐτῷ ἔχῃ ζωὴν αἰώνιον.", "16": "Οὕτως γὰρ ἠγάπησεν ὁ θεὸς τὸν κόσμον ὥστε τὸν υἱὸν τὸν μονογενῆ ἔδωκεν, ἵνα πᾶς ὁ πιστεύων εἰς αὐτὸν μὴ ἀπόληται ἀλλὰ ἔχῃ ζωὴν αἰώνιον.", "17": "οὐ γὰρ ἀπέστειλεν ὁ θεὸς τὸν υἱὸν εἰς τὸν κόσμον ἵνα κρίνῃ τὸν κόσμον, ἀλλ’ ἵνα σωθῇ ὁ κόσμος δι’ αὐτοῦ."}, "source_url": "https://github.com/morphgnt/sblgnt/blob/master/64-Jn-morphgnt.txt", "license_url": "https://sblgnt.com/license/"}}, "matt16_18": {"web": {"name": "World English Bible", "note": "Public Domain", "verses": {"13": "Now when Jesus came into the parts of Caesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples, saying, “Who do men say that I, the Son of Man, am?”", "14": "They said, “Some say John the Baptizer, some, Elijah, and others, Jeremiah, or one of the prophets.”", "15": "He said to them, “But who do you say that I am?”", "16": "Simon Peter answered, “You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.”", "17": "Jesus answered him, “Blessed are you, Simon Bar Jonah, for flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven.", "18": "I also tell you that you are Peter, and on this rock I will build my assembly, and the gates of Hades will not prevail against it.", "19": "I will give to you the keys of the Kingdom of Heaven, and whatever you bind on earth will have been bound in heaven; and whatever you release on earth will have been released in heaven.”"}, "source_url": "https://bible-api.com/matthew+16:13-19?translation=web"}, "kjv": {"name": "King James Version", "note": "Public Domain", "verses": {"13": "When Jesus came into the coasts of Caesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples, saying, Whom do men say that I the Son of man am?", "14": "And they said, Some say that thou art John the Baptist: some, Elias; and others, Jeremias, or one of the prophets.", "15": "He saith unto them, But whom say ye that I am?", "16": "And Simon Peter answered and said, Thou art the Christ, the Son of the living God.", "17": "And Jesus answered and said unto him, Blessed art thou, Simon Barjona: for flesh and blood hath not revealed it unto thee, but my Father which is in heaven.", "18": "And I say also unto thee, That thou art Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.", "19": "And I will give unto thee the keys of the kingdom of heaven: and whatsoever thou shalt bind on earth shall be bound in heaven: and whatsoever thou shalt loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven."}, "source_url": "https://bible-api.com/matthew+16:13-19?translation=kjv"}, "asv": {"name": "American Standard Version (1901)", "note": "Public Domain", "verses": {"13": "Now when Jesus came into the parts of Caesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples, saying, Who do men say that the Son of man is?", "14": "And they said, Some [say] John the Baptist; some, Elijah; and others, Jeremiah, or one of the prophets.", "15": "He saith unto them, But who say ye that I am?", "16": "And Simon Peter answered and said, Thou art the Christ, the Son of the living God.", "17": "And Jesus answered and said unto him, Blessed art thou, Simon Bar-jonah: for flesh and blood hath not revealed it unto thee, but my Father who is in heaven.", "18": "And I also say unto thee, that thou art Peter, and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of Hades shall not prevail against it.", "19": "I will give unto thee the keys of the kingdom of heaven: and whatsoever thou shalt bind on earth shall be bound in heaven; and whatsoever thou shalt loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven."}, "source_url": "https://bible-api.com/matthew+16:13-19?translation=asv"}, "sblgnt": {"name": "SBL Greek New Testament (SBLGNT)", "note": "CC BY 4.0, Society of Biblical Literature and Logos Bible Software; text via MorphGNT (Tauber ed.), critical sigla removed", "verses": {"13": "Ἐλθὼν δὲ ὁ Ἰησοῦς εἰς τὰ μέρη Καισαρείας τῆς Φιλίππου ἠρώτα τοὺς μαθητὰς αὐτοῦ λέγων· Τίνα λέγουσιν οἱ ἄνθρωποι εἶναι τὸν υἱὸν τοῦ ἀνθρώπου;", "14": "οἱ δὲ εἶπαν· Οἱ μὲν Ἰωάννην τὸν βαπτιστήν, ἄλλοι δὲ Ἠλίαν, ἕτεροι δὲ Ἰερεμίαν ἢ ἕνα τῶν προφητῶν.", "15": "λέγει αὐτοῖς· Ὑμεῖς δὲ τίνα με λέγετε εἶναι;", "16": "ἀποκριθεὶς δὲ Σίμων Πέτρος εἶπεν· Σὺ εἶ ὁ χριστὸς ὁ υἱὸς τοῦ θεοῦ τοῦ ζῶντος.", "17": "ἀποκριθεὶς δὲ ὁ Ἰησοῦς εἶπεν αὐτῷ· Μακάριος εἶ, Σίμων Βαριωνᾶ, ὅτι σὰρξ καὶ αἷμα οὐκ ἀπεκάλυψέν σοι ἀλλ’ ὁ πατήρ μου ὁ ἐν τοῖς οὐρανοῖς·", "18": "κἀγὼ δέ σοι λέγω ὅτι σὺ εἶ Πέτρος, καὶ ἐπὶ ταύτῃ τῇ πέτρᾳ οἰκοδομήσω μου τὴν ἐκκλησίαν, καὶ πύλαι ᾅδου οὐ κατισχύσουσιν αὐτῆς·", "19": "δώσω σοι τὰς κλεῖδας τῆς βασιλείας τῶν οὐρανῶν, καὶ ὃ ἐὰν δήσῃς ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς ἔσται δεδεμένον ἐν τοῖς οὐρανοῖς, καὶ ὃ ἐὰν λύσῃς ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς ἔσται λελυμένον ἐν τοῖς οὐρανοῖς."}, "source_url": "https://github.com/morphgnt/sblgnt/blob/master/61-Mt-morphgnt.txt", "license_url": "https://sblgnt.com/license/"}}}};
